Z||000007||StuGood||07-11-2000||01:28 AM||||I might suggest you check the throttle position sensor (TPS) first.  Reasons? 1)It  provides an "accelerator pump" function, which is what you'd check first on a non-fuel injected car, and  2)It's so easy to get to and diagnose.<P>If you suspect tach filter, just disconnect it. If the stalling problem goes away, then you'll know why  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!  <P>If you are certain lack of fuel is the problem, then next on (my) list would be fuel filter (fairly easy to replace); followed by the fuel pump.  Test the pressure / delivery of the fuel pump if possible, before you go to the trouble of replacing it.<P>Hope this helps you, and good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!   ||||reg||3
Z||000008||Pretender||07-11-2000||05:30 PM||||i installed a new tps and a fuel filter about 5 months ago when i had a tune up. where is the tach filter located? ive noticed that on a few days even when it's warm outside and i have my turnsignal on at idle(stoplight) the tach actually moves with the turn signall along with the volt meter. <P>what does the tach filter do? it's purpose? thanks for anwsering my other questions, i'm glad you guys are taking the time to help.||||reg||10
Z||000009||StuGood||07-11-2000||06:30 PM||||Tach filter is located near the ignition coil.  It looks like an ignition condenser - a little silver can - except it has a white wire on BOTH ends.  To test, unplug it and see if your problems go away (you lose tachometer for the duration of the test).<P>Our fine moderator Cliff informs us that the tach filter is 2 low-pass filters in series.  Apparently it cleans up the signal from the ignition coil, before it is passed onto the tach circuitry.<P>If you do a search using "tach filter" in subject line, you'll see more threads on the subject.<P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !       ||||reg||1
Z||000010||btoth||07-11-2000||09:54 PM||||my car almost stalled every time i would hit the gas too... turned out to be an old worn out catalytic converter.  caused too much back pressure.||||reg||3
Z||000011||Pretender||07-12-2000||03:03 PM||||i know it can't be a cat. converter somwhere in the whole upgradeable exhaust system i think it dissapeared, but i know i'll be buying one in the future becausei can see pennsylvania going the emisions way like maryland and the other states. thanks for the tach filter info, i'll be testing it today and i'll see if that is my prob...||||reg||12
